<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eumenes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/241-bce">241 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eumenes I was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> from 263 to 241 BCE, the nephew and adopted heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philetaerus">Philetaerus</a>. He defeated the Seleucid king Antiochus I near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> around 261 BCE, making Pergamon independent and extending its territory, though he never took the title king. He was succeeded by his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a>, the first Attalid to call himself king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eumenes-i.jpg" alt="Eumenes I" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmenesICoin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
